  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Where a signed write's time actually goes",
      "plan_item": "§6.3",
      "verdict": "refuted",
      "facts": {
        "claim_to_verify_ratio": 21.78,
        "durable_claim_p50_ms": 4.01,
        "rounds": 40,
        "unauthenticated_read_p50_ms": 0.08,
        "verify_and_quota_p50_ms": 0.18,
        "verify_plus_durable_claim_p50_ms": 4.19
      },
      "notes": [
        "The two signed measurements are the same envelope on the same route from the same key. They differ by exactly one step: the first is claimed and fsynced before the handler refuses it, the second is caught by the in-memory replay set at the claim and never written."
      ],
      "findings": [
        "§6 item 3 names signature-verify CPU as the third thing to break, but verification and the quota charge together cost 0.18ms while the durable replay claim behind them costs 4.01ms -- 22x more. Signed writes are bounded by one fsync per request, not by ECDSA. That also means the write path does not scale with cores, and that batching or group-committing the replay guard's writes is worth more than anything done to the verify."
      ],
